 34AL cG E,RI$from 2013StZY.                                                    
 34AL cG $2013StZY observed a 658|g that appears predominantly in the time 0-10 
 34AL2cG ms after an implantation of {+36}Mg. 2013StZY states that the 658|g is 
 34AL3cG a prompt line, so it must originate from deexcitation in one of the    
 34AL4cG daughter nuclei {+34,35,36}Al, and it is very likely that the 658|g    
 34AL5cG corresponds to the 657|g observed in 2001Pr08 via                      
 34AL6cG {+197}Au({+34}Al,{+34}Al'|g) Coulomb excitation.                       
 34AL cG $2023Lu07 observed a 657|g within the 100-ms window following the      
 34AL2cG arrival of a {+36}Mg implantation in both NSCL and FRIB experiments.   
 34AL3cG 2023Lu07 states that if the 657|g belongs to {+34}Al, the probability  
 34AL4cG of two-neutron emission would need to be significantly larger than that
 34AL5cG of one-neutron emission based on the intensities of the relevant       
 34AL6cG transitions in the |g-ray spectrum. They assign the 657|g to {+36}Al,  
 34AL7cG though some possible contributions from {+34}Al cannot be ruled out.   

